1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,060
On the morning of June tenth, nineteen twelve,

2
00:00:03,760 --> 00:00:05,919
a mother in southwest Iowa

3
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:08,140
telephoned a neighbor's house.

4
00:00:08,839 --> 00:00:10,000
Her husband told the coroner about it.

5
00:00:10,320 --> 00:00:11,259
My wife did, yes.

6
00:00:12,080 --> 00:00:15,339
He could not say what time.

7
00:00:16,019 --> 00:00:17,080
She tried, he said,

8
00:00:17,760 --> 00:00:18,360
about three different times.

9
00:00:18,240 --> 00:00:22,500
Her two daughters had stayed the night there.

10
00:00:23,900 --> 00:00:25,199
She expected them back.

11
00:00:26,100 --> 00:00:26,859
That house was locked.

12
00:00:27,219 --> 00:00:27,900
The blinds were down.

13
00:00:28,620 --> 00:00:30,800
Everyone who tells this story

14
00:00:31,060 --> 00:00:33,560
will tell you why it was never solved.

15
00:00:34,020 --> 00:00:36,880
A crowd walked through the rooms.

16
00:00:37,079 --> 00:00:37,880
The evidence was lost.

17
00:00:38,579 --> 00:00:41,460
And that is partly true.

18
00:00:42,160 --> 00:00:44,500
But here is what the paperwork shows.

19
00:00:45,219 --> 00:00:47,780
This case did not run out of evidence.

20
00:00:48,520 --> 00:00:49,560
For nineteen years

21
00:00:50,460 --> 00:00:51,399
it kept getting more.

22
00:00:52,079 --> 00:00:52,679
A closet.

23
00:00:53,340 --> 00:00:57,060
A photograph, said to come from inside a child's eye.

24
00:00:57,780 --> 00:01:00,039
A sound that turned into a man.

25
00:01:00,719 --> 00:01:01,859
A signed confession.

26
00:01:02,539 --> 00:01:03,280
A fingerprint.

27
00:01:03,980 --> 00:01:07,760
I can't tell you who killed eight people in Villisca.

28
00:01:08,460 --> 00:01:09,140
No one can.

29
00:01:09,820 --> 00:01:11,980
What I can give you is an inventory.

30
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:13,260
What was in that house.

31
00:01:14,019 --> 00:01:16,320
And what was carried in afterward.

32
00:01:16,958 --> 00:01:18,718
The first entry is the people.

33
00:01:19,278 --> 00:01:20,578
Joe Moore was forty-three.

34
00:01:21,118 --> 00:01:22,478
He sold farm implements.

35
00:01:23,058 --> 00:01:24,578
Sarah Moore was thirty-nine,

36
00:01:25,118 --> 00:01:27,758
and she helped run the Children's Day program

37
00:01:28,378 --> 00:01:29,778
at the Presbyterian church.

38
00:01:30,358 --> 00:01:30,958
Herman was eleven.

39
00:01:31,358 --> 00:01:32,118
That Sunday night,

40
00:01:32,658 --> 00:01:34,958
he spoke a piece

41
00:01:35,538 --> 00:01:38,458
about God watching over the sparrows.

42
00:01:39,018 --> 00:01:39,618
Katherine was ten.

43
00:01:39,798 --> 00:01:40,438
Boyd was seven.

44
00:01:40,978 --> 00:01:41,818
Paul was five.

45
00:01:42,378 --> 00:01:47,258
Lena Stillinger took part in the program too.

46
00:01:48,158 --> 00:01:49,118
Her sister Ina was with her.

47
00:01:49,618 --> 00:01:50,478
The records disagree about their ages.

48
00:01:51,058 --> 00:01:53,298
Lena was eleven or twelve.

49
00:01:53,878 --> 00:01:56,058
Ina was eight or nine,

50
00:01:56,618 --> 00:01:58,418
and one trial report says six.

51
00:01:58,958 --> 00:02:00,498
At six o'clock that evening,

52
00:02:00,778 --> 00:02:03,078
according to their older sister Blanche,

53
00:02:03,618 --> 00:02:05,458
Joe Moore telephoned the Stillinger home.

54
00:02:06,018 --> 00:02:10,638
He did not want the girls walking back in the dark.

55
00:02:10,878 --> 00:02:12,618
Could they stay with his family?

56
00:02:13,238 --> 00:02:16,458
Blanche said she thought that would be all right.

57
00:02:17,058 --> 00:02:19,058
The pastor testified the program ended about nine fifteen.

58
00:02:19,398 --> 00:02:21,058
Nobody who saw what came next

59
00:02:21,598 --> 00:02:25,058
ever gave an account that held.

60
00:02:25,458 --> 00:02:27,658
So I won't give you one.

61
00:02:28,291 --> 00:02:28,931
Monday morning.

62
00:02:29,591 --> 00:02:31,491
A neighbor, Mary Peckham,

63
00:02:32,191 --> 00:02:33,111
noticed how still the house was.

64
00:02:33,391 --> 00:02:34,511
She tried the doors.

65
00:02:35,231 --> 00:02:38,091
Joe's brother Ross came with a key.

66
00:02:38,511 --> 00:02:41,991
He opened the door to the room off the parlor.

67
00:02:42,691 --> 00:02:43,431
He told the coroner

68
00:02:44,131 --> 00:02:46,871
he did not wait long enough to see anything else.

69
00:02:47,591 --> 00:02:50,851
The town marshal, Hank Horton, went through every room.

70
00:02:51,531 --> 00:02:52,971
And then he did something

71
00:02:53,671 --> 00:02:54,871
almost nobody retells.

72
00:02:55,291 --> 00:02:55,931
According to the Moore store clerk,

73
00:02:56,631 --> 00:02:58,351
he locked the house

74
00:02:59,131 --> 00:03:02,971
and left the keys with the neighbor.

75
00:03:03,651 --> 00:03:04,251
Remember that.

76
00:03:02,771 --> 00:03:04,211
A locked house.

77
00:03:04,651 --> 00:03:07,151
A woman next door, holding the key.

78
00:03:07,691 --> 00:03:11,211
It is the last moment anyone could say

79
00:03:11,891 --> 00:03:12,851
who had been inside.

80
00:03:13,631 --> 00:03:14,651
When the doctors went in,

81
00:03:15,331 --> 00:03:16,431
every face was covered.

82
00:03:17,231 --> 00:03:18,111
Dr. Williams climbed the stairs

83
00:03:18,391 --> 00:03:21,751
to the room on the south side.

84
00:03:22,551 --> 00:03:24,431
A small boy, on his stomach.

85
00:03:25,211 --> 00:03:26,231
A gauze undershirt

86
00:03:26,991 --> 00:03:28,611
laid over the top of his head.

87
00:03:29,331 --> 00:03:33,451
Dr. Williams told the inquest what he did next.

88
00:03:34,351 --> 00:03:35,291
I lifted that off

89
00:03:36,191 --> 00:03:36,931
lifted it off

90
00:03:37,831 --> 00:03:39,051
to see which one it was.

91
00:03:44,749 --> 00:03:46,249
So here is the first inventory.

92
00:03:46,689 --> 00:03:51,229
An ax, near the bed where Lena and Ina were found.

93
00:03:51,689 --> 00:03:54,309
A piece of bacon, wrapped in a flour sack,

94
00:03:54,809 --> 00:03:57,769
at the head of that bed.

95
00:03:58,209 --> 00:04:00,429
A lamp on the floor, at the foot of the parents' bed.

96
00:04:00,849 --> 00:04:04,869
The marshal moved it out of the way.

97
00:04:05,269 --> 00:04:07,149
Skirts and aprons hung over the windows.

98
00:04:07,609 --> 00:04:08,589
Marks in the ceiling.

99
00:04:09,029 --> 00:04:10,149
Dr. Williams found no footprints.

100
00:04:10,589 --> 00:04:11,809
Five years later,

101
00:04:12,109 --> 00:04:12,909
the coroner testified

102
00:04:13,349 --> 00:04:14,689
there were no finger marks on the ax handle.

103
00:04:15,149 --> 00:04:17,709
Then the house was opened again.

104
00:04:18,109 --> 00:04:20,569
The National Guard came to hold the line.

105
00:04:20,989 --> 00:04:22,089
One account says ten thirty.

106
00:04:22,509 --> 00:04:24,429
Another says noon.

107
00:04:24,849 --> 00:04:28,089
How many people went in before that, I can't tell you.

108
00:04:28,349 --> 00:04:30,429
Later accounts say one hundred.

109
00:04:30,889 --> 00:04:32,729
Others say hundreds.

110
00:04:33,149 --> 00:04:34,250
I have not found a count from that day.

111
00:04:34,709 --> 00:04:36,529
What the record does show

112
00:04:36,769 --> 00:04:38,989
is a house entered again and again.

113
00:04:39,549 --> 00:04:43,209
Jessie Moore testified she went in that day,

114
00:04:43,649 --> 00:04:44,629
to get family pictures for the press.

115
00:04:45,069 --> 00:04:46,189
Bedding was burned.

116
00:04:46,809 --> 00:04:48,589
The house was cleaned, then repapered.

117
00:04:49,029 --> 00:04:49,769
So yes.

118
00:04:50,229 --> 00:04:50,870
Things were lost.

119
00:04:50,870 --> 00:04:52,349
But loss only explains what disappeared.

120
00:04:53,029 --> 00:04:55,349
It cannot explain what showed up.

121
00:04:56,083 --> 00:04:59,723
Item one arrives five days after the murders.

122
00:05:00,163 --> 00:05:01,223
A newspaper report, datelined Omaha.

123
00:05:01,743 --> 00:05:03,183
A woman named Retta Johnson

124
00:05:03,623 --> 00:05:05,383
has come back from Villisca.

125
00:05:05,843 --> 00:05:09,483
There is a closet, she says,

126
00:05:09,923 --> 00:05:13,903
off the room where the Stillinger girls slept.

127
00:05:14,423 --> 00:05:16,243
Bags of cotton batting inside.

128
00:05:16,703 --> 00:05:18,343
On them, the marks of a man

129
00:05:18,843 --> 00:05:20,943
who had sat and stood there.

130
00:05:21,383 --> 00:05:22,963
And in the girls' bed,

131
00:05:23,403 --> 00:05:24,503
a piece of watch chain.

132
00:05:24,943 --> 00:05:26,203
Now follow the closet.

133
00:05:26,623 --> 00:05:29,023
By the trial, five years on,

134
00:05:29,423 --> 00:05:31,823
the list of exhibits names a closet upstairs.

135
00:05:32,323 --> 00:05:37,003
A public comment posted under historian Edgar Epperly's name

136
00:05:37,363 --> 00:05:39,823
traces a version of the story to a Kansas City reporter,

137
00:05:40,303 --> 00:05:44,363
with cigarettes, and a heel print in the dust.

138
00:05:44,943 --> 00:05:46,203
It says an undertaker checked,

139
00:05:46,723 --> 00:05:51,043
and the box of cotton sat on two suit boxes

140
00:05:51,603 --> 00:05:53,423
that had not been crushed.

141
00:05:53,883 --> 00:05:57,843
Today, you will hear it was the attic.

142
00:05:58,223 --> 00:05:59,023
Now follow the chain.

143
00:05:59,483 --> 00:06:04,283
A watch chain, in a bed, in June.

144
00:06:04,703 --> 00:06:05,563
By the trial,

145
00:06:06,003 --> 00:06:07,843
a piece of key chain,

146
00:06:08,243 --> 00:06:11,083
and Joe Moore's watch, on the floor.

147
00:06:11,543 --> 00:06:13,203
The containers stayed the same.

148
00:06:13,883 --> 00:06:16,203
What was inside them kept changing.

149
00:06:16,833 --> 00:06:18,113
August, nineteen twelve.

150
00:06:18,853 --> 00:06:21,513
A man from Villisca tells people in Council Bluffs

151
00:06:22,333 --> 00:06:25,213
that detectives have a photograph of the killer.

152
00:06:25,933 --> 00:06:27,753
They got it, he says,

153
00:06:28,153 --> 00:06:33,953
from the retina of one of the Stillinger sisters.

154
00:06:34,153 --> 00:06:35,753
I have found no such photograph in any record.

155
00:06:36,453 --> 00:06:40,133
Her parents were alive to read that.

156
00:06:40,873 --> 00:06:42,533
By the end of that year,

157
00:06:43,433 --> 00:06:48,233
newspapers had reported at least five different men held or arrested.

158
00:06:48,493 --> 00:06:49,093
A brother-in-law.

159
00:06:49,113 --> 00:06:51,813
A man with stained shoes.

160
00:06:52,493 --> 00:06:54,993
A farmhand who wrote about a vision.

161
00:06:55,693 --> 00:06:58,353
A Sioux City porter,

162
00:06:59,053 --> 00:07:01,193
whom the headline identified by his race.

163
00:07:01,433 --> 00:07:02,713
A farmer.

164
00:07:02,713 --> 00:07:07,953
None of them was convicted of anything at Villisca.

165
00:07:08,293 --> 00:07:11,613
The question had stopped being what happened in the house.

166
00:07:11,873 --> 00:07:13,033
It had become

167
00:07:13,753 --> 00:07:16,513
who was going to pay for it.

168
00:07:17,166 --> 00:07:18,626
In nineteen sixteen,

169
00:07:19,026 --> 00:07:20,166
a private detective named James Wilkerson

170
00:07:20,466 --> 00:07:21,766
brought the case a theory.

171
00:07:22,146 --> 00:07:23,286
State senator Frank Jones,

172
00:07:23,726 --> 00:07:27,166
Joe Moore's old employer and business rival,

173
00:07:27,566 --> 00:07:28,626
had hired a killer.

174
00:07:29,046 --> 00:07:29,826
Wilkerson's man was William Mansfield.

175
00:07:30,246 --> 00:07:32,886
A grand jury refused to indict him.

176
00:07:33,326 --> 00:07:36,386
Frank Jones was never charged with anything.

177
00:07:36,626 --> 00:07:37,586
He sued Wilkerson for slander,

178
00:07:37,926 --> 00:07:40,766
asked for sixty thousand dollars,

179
00:07:41,166 --> 00:07:41,766
and lost.

180
00:07:40,526 --> 00:07:42,866
And in that lawsuit,

181
00:07:43,286 --> 00:07:45,486
a witness named Ed Landers took the oath.

182
00:07:45,966 --> 00:07:46,626
You have met Landers already,

183
00:07:46,886 --> 00:07:48,626
in a way.

184
00:07:49,166 --> 00:07:53,526
He was staying a few houses from the Moores.

185
00:07:53,826 --> 00:07:54,526
At the inquest in nineteen twelve,

186
00:07:54,946 --> 00:07:59,146
he described something he heard that Sunday night.

187
00:07:59,646 --> 00:08:00,246
A sound.

188
00:07:58,846 --> 00:08:01,726
Like one boy hooting for another.

189
00:08:02,466 --> 00:08:06,286
The next day, he said, it occurred to him

190
00:08:06,946 --> 00:08:09,566
it might have been a woman moaning.

191
00:08:09,986 --> 00:08:12,086
In the published summary of that testimony,

192
00:08:12,506 --> 00:08:13,606
that is all there is.

193
00:08:14,046 --> 00:08:14,846
Four years later,

194
00:08:15,246 --> 00:08:15,846
under oath,

195
00:08:16,266 --> 00:08:18,666
he said that on that same night

196
00:08:19,086 --> 00:08:21,366
he saw Frank Jones's son, Albert,

197
00:08:21,866 --> 00:08:24,926
go into the Moore home.

198
00:08:26,326 --> 00:08:27,806
Albert Jones was never charged either.

199
00:08:28,246 --> 00:08:29,866
Both men denied any part in it.

200
00:08:30,306 --> 00:08:34,146
The prosecution attacked Landers's account in open court.

201
00:08:34,586 --> 00:08:35,406
A sound

202
00:08:36,306 --> 00:08:37,006
became a man.

203
00:08:42,666 --> 00:08:46,386
In nineteen seventeen, the state found its own document.

204
00:08:46,946 --> 00:08:47,646
The Reverend Lyn George Kelly

205
00:08:47,846 --> 00:08:49,186
was a traveling preacher,

206
00:08:49,586 --> 00:08:52,566
in town for one Sunday.

207
00:08:52,986 --> 00:08:54,546
He was charged with the murder of Lena Stillinger.

208
00:08:54,786 --> 00:08:58,706
He had been charged with mailing obscene letters,

209
00:08:59,146 --> 00:09:02,066
and he had been held in a federal mental hospital.

210
00:09:02,486 --> 00:09:04,486
He caught the five nineteen train that morning.

211
00:09:05,206 --> 00:09:08,066
The train left hours before anyone opened the door.

212
00:09:08,386 --> 00:09:09,946
Witnesses swore he spoke of the murders

213
00:09:10,386 --> 00:09:12,686
before the bodies were found.

214
00:09:13,106 --> 00:09:14,566
They swore it five years later.

215
00:09:15,086 --> 00:09:19,106
A station ticket record backed up the date of one encounter.

216
00:09:19,386 --> 00:09:22,206
It could not record what was said.

217
00:09:22,626 --> 00:09:23,226
Then, Logan jail.

218
00:09:23,266 --> 00:09:23,886
August thirtieth.

219
00:09:24,306 --> 00:09:25,106
A newspaper editor testified

220
00:09:25,506 --> 00:09:29,066
that he was handcuffed and placed in Kelly's cell,

221
00:09:29,346 --> 00:09:30,686
playing an automobile thief.

222
00:09:31,106 --> 00:09:32,346
A sheriff read out accusations.

223
00:09:32,766 --> 00:09:34,486
The editor recalled eighty-seven paragraphs.

224
00:09:34,946 --> 00:09:35,926
Kelly said,

225
00:09:36,226 --> 00:09:37,666
that's a lie.

226
00:09:38,106 --> 00:09:41,226
Early the next morning, a county attorney took down a statement.

227
00:09:41,626 --> 00:09:43,446
Its first lines say it was made

228
00:09:44,206 --> 00:09:45,926
without any promise or threats.

229
00:09:46,346 --> 00:09:50,346
In it, a voice tells Kelly to slay utterly.

230
00:09:50,646 --> 00:09:53,466
He climbs the stairs, thinking they are Jacob's ladder.

231
00:09:53,786 --> 00:09:55,326
He kills the children first, it says.

232
00:09:55,766 --> 00:09:57,746
Then the parents.

233
00:09:58,166 --> 00:10:00,726
No record I have read can tell you

234
00:10:01,146 --> 00:10:04,026
whether that is the order it happened.

235
00:10:04,746 --> 00:10:06,166
The attorney general told the jury

236
00:10:06,586 --> 00:10:09,466
Kelly had been asked fifteen times

237
00:10:09,886 --> 00:10:11,386
whether he killed the parents first.

238
00:10:11,846 --> 00:10:14,046
And fifteen times, he said no.

239
00:10:14,446 --> 00:10:17,826
Twenty-five times, he was told no promise could be made.

240
00:10:18,226 --> 00:10:21,566
Fifty-one times, he was told to tell the truth.

241
00:10:22,026 --> 00:10:23,086
The state counted those.

242
00:10:23,526 --> 00:10:27,206
It offered them as proof that he was free.

243
00:10:27,586 --> 00:10:28,186
At trial, Kelly said

244
00:10:28,566 --> 00:10:33,046
I have no recollection of signing that paper.

245
00:10:33,286 --> 00:10:34,246
The record shows a signature.

246
00:10:34,946 --> 00:10:38,406
It cannot show you what the signature is worth.

247
00:10:39,166 --> 00:10:42,186
There is one more name in the Omaha paper.

248
00:10:42,926 --> 00:10:45,226
A committee was raising money for Kelly's defense.

249
00:10:46,006 --> 00:10:46,866
It held meetings.

250
00:10:47,766 --> 00:10:49,306
It passed the hat.

251
00:10:50,086 --> 00:10:50,826
Its officers are listed.

252
00:10:51,566 --> 00:10:52,906
Remember the man who told the coroner

253
00:10:53,666 --> 00:10:55,546
my wife did, yes.

254
00:10:56,286 --> 00:10:57,366
She phoned about three different times.

255
00:10:58,126 --> 00:11:01,606
The state of Iowa had charged Kelly

256
00:11:01,846 --> 00:11:02,706
with killing his daughter.

257
00:11:04,106 --> 00:11:05,166
And Joseph Stillinger

258
00:11:06,066 --> 00:11:09,186
was the president of the committee

259
00:11:09,946 --> 00:11:10,766
trying to set him free.

260
00:11:16,458 --> 00:11:17,858
The paper does not say why.

261
00:11:18,338 --> 00:11:22,018
The committee said it wanted the guilty man brought to justice.

262
00:11:22,478 --> 00:11:25,438
It did not believe it had him.

263
00:11:25,878 --> 00:11:28,498
The first jury took twenty ballots.

264
00:11:29,018 --> 00:11:29,738
Eleven for acquittal, every time.

265
00:11:30,178 --> 00:11:35,938
One man, W. C. Brown, held for not guilty by reason of insanity.

266
00:11:36,398 --> 00:11:39,578
In November, a second jury acquitted Kelly.

267
00:11:40,038 --> 00:11:40,998
He was found not guilty.

268
00:11:41,258 --> 00:11:43,138
That is the verdict.

269
00:11:43,738 --> 00:11:45,158
There is one last item.

270
00:11:45,598 --> 00:11:47,218
March, nineteen thirty-one.

271
00:11:47,618 --> 00:11:48,278
A man in a Detroit jail,

272
00:11:48,698 --> 00:11:49,778
after five hours with detectives,

273
00:11:50,218 --> 00:11:53,518
confesses to the Villisca murders.

274
00:11:53,838 --> 00:11:54,858
The papers report

275
00:11:55,278 --> 00:11:59,538
that his fingerprints matched prints found at the scene.

276
00:11:59,938 --> 00:12:00,538
Remember the coroner.

277
00:11:59,358 --> 00:12:02,518
No finger marks on the handle.

278
00:12:02,938 --> 00:12:04,498
And this man's confession has a problem.

279
00:12:04,898 --> 00:12:07,098
He says he killed a man,

280
00:12:07,538 --> 00:12:08,138
his wife,

281
00:12:08,618 --> 00:12:11,138
and their four children.

282
00:12:11,558 --> 00:12:12,178
Six people.

283
00:12:12,878 --> 00:12:14,998
Officers said it did not tally.

284
00:12:15,698 --> 00:12:17,118
The last confession in the file

285
00:12:18,018 --> 00:12:20,098
forgot Lena and Ina.

286
00:12:25,791 --> 00:12:27,851
So here is the inventory now.

287
00:12:28,591 --> 00:12:29,231
A house.

288
00:12:30,011 --> 00:12:30,611
An ax.

289
00:12:31,331 --> 00:12:32,211
A few photographs.

290
00:12:32,951 --> 00:12:34,351
Everything else was carried in.

291
00:12:35,111 --> 00:12:38,891
Some of it, by people with something to gain.

292
00:12:39,331 --> 00:12:41,351
Much of it, I think,

293
00:12:41,671 --> 00:12:46,591
by people who could not stand an empty line on the form.

294
00:12:47,351 --> 00:12:48,571
That is what this kind of crime does.

295
00:12:49,071 --> 00:12:52,951
It leaves a space the exact size of a name.

296
00:12:53,691 --> 00:12:57,871
And a town will put anything in it.

297
00:12:58,631 --> 00:12:59,231
A stranger.

298
00:12:59,531 --> 00:13:00,131
A senator.

299
00:12:59,931 --> 00:13:01,831
A preacher who heard voices.

300
00:13:02,291 --> 00:13:05,711
No one has ever been convicted of these murders.

301
00:13:06,131 --> 00:13:06,971
Reverend Kelly was acquitted.

302
00:13:07,431 --> 00:13:10,631
Frank and Albert Jones were never charged.

303
00:13:10,991 --> 00:13:12,731
The one thing in this file

304
00:13:13,011 --> 00:13:15,271
that nobody needed to invent

305
00:13:15,991 --> 00:13:16,891
is a mother,

306
00:13:17,671 --> 00:13:18,271
on a Monday morning,

307
00:13:18,151 --> 00:13:19,331
calling a house.

308
00:13:20,231 --> 00:13:21,731
About three different times.

309
00:13:21,731 --> 00:13:27,591
This film is drawn from court testimony and newspapers of the time.

310
00:13:28,031 --> 00:13:29,371
A report is not proof.

311
00:13:29,851 --> 00:13:32,811
Everyone named here is presumed innocent of these murders.
